ASCEBC - translate ASCII character string to EBCDIC
       CALL ASCEBC(LENGTH,BUFF1<,BUFF2>)
       where
       LENGTH is the length in bytes of the ASCII string to be translated.
       BUF1 is source string. If BUF2 is not specified, it will also contain the
              output EBCDIC string.  BUFF1 should be  DIMENSIONed to be at least
              LENGTH bytes long.
       BUF2 is  an  optional  string  to contain  the  EBCDIC  output  and,   if
              specified, should be at least length bytes long.

CLEAR  - clear a 3270 screen.
       CALL CLEAR

CMSARG - pass a parameter list to a FORTRAN program.
       CALL CMSARG (PARM1,PARM2,...)
       where:  PARM1,PARM2,...  are REAL*8 variables  to contain the parameters.
       CMS modules  generally are able  to accept  an invocation arglist  in the
       form of a tokenized parameter list.  FORTRAN main programs, however, lack
       the  capability of  accepting invocation  parameters.    This routine  is
       intended to be  called from a FORTRAN  main program in order  to retrieve
       the invocation PLIST,  and to pass the parameters Each element in the CMS
       PLIST is passed to the location specified by the corresponding element in
       the FORTRAN argument list.   If the PLIST is shorter, unused locations in
       the FORTRAN list are filled with ZFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.   If the FORTRAN list
       is shorter, the remaining PLIST elements are not transferred.   Note that
       the first PLIST element is the command name.

CORE - in-core formatted READ/WRITE.
       CALL CORE(A,LENGTH)
       A      is an array  name defining the buffer  to be read from  or written
              into.   After CORE  is called,   the  next FORTRAN  READ or  WRITE
              statement executed will be treated as a dummy and the data will be
              read  from or  written to  the buffer  A according  to the  format
              specified in the READ or WRITE statement.
       LENGTH is the integer length in bytes of the buffer A.
                                    __ _____
              Example 1 stores variables A, I and B(4) into array X using FORMAT
              1 then reads the data back into variables IA1,  IA2,  K,  CODE and
              B(1) through B(10) according to FORMAT 2.
                REAL*8 CODE
                DIMENSION X(30), B(10)
                ..
                CALL CORE(X,120)
                WRITE(20,1) A, I, (B(4), L=1,8)
          1     FORMAT(F10.4, I2, 3X, 'ARRAY', 8A4)
                CALL CORE(X,120)
                READ 2, IA1, IA2, K, CODE, (B(L), L=1,10)
          2     FORMAT(I5, 1X, I4, I2, A8, 10(A1, 2X))
                ..
       Example 2 uses CORE to re-read data  from an input data card according to
       a number specified on the card.
                DIMENSION DATA(19)
                ..
          100   READ 1, NF, (DATA(I), I=1,19)
          1     FORMAT(I4, 19A4)
                GO TO (101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 106), NF
                ...
          104   CALL CORE(DATA, 76)
                READ(1, 2) ND, A, B, C, D
          2     FORMAT(I6, 4F10.0)
                ..

DATTIM - get DATE and TIME of day.
       CALL DATTIM(DATE,TIME)
       DATE  is a REAL*8 variable that will  contain the eight character date in
              MM/DD/YY format.
       TIME is a REAL*8  variable that will contain the eight  character time in
              HH:MM:SS format.

DIAG - issue a CP diagnose command.
       CALL DIAG(CODE, Rx, Rx+1, Ry, Ry+1, R15, &1, &2, &3)
       where
       CODE is function as defined in VM/SP System Programmer's Guide.
       Rx, Rx+1, Ry, Ry+1 are the arguments to DIAG.
       R15 is set to the value of R15 at end  of diagnose.   The value in R15 is
              also returned as an integer function result when DIAG is called as
              a function.
       &1, &2, &3 are returns taken depending on condition code set by diagnose:
              0=RETURN, 1..3=RETURN n.
The argument list may  be truncated on the right of  all unneeded arguments (e.g
R15).   Since all arguments present are set to  a new value at the conclusion of
the DIAG instruction there should be only variables in the formal parameter list
not constants.   Unneeded parameters (e.g.  Rx+1) should not be constants either
but rather a dummy integer value (e.g. IDUM1).
Example:
       INTEGER IAD,LEN/160/          length of buffer
       REAL*8 AREA(20)               buffer
       IAD=IADDR(AREA)               address of buffer
       CALL DIAG(0,IAD,IDUM1,LEN)    DIAG X'00' - store extended
                                                  identification

       INTEGER RX,RX1,RY,RY1
       LOGICAL*1 COMMAND(7),RETURN(8192),RYFLAG
       EQUIVALENCE (RYFLAG,RY)
       DATA /'Q','U','E','R','Y',' ','P'/
       RX=IADDR(COMMAND)
       RX1=IADDR(RETURN)
       RY=7
       RYFLAG=64
       RY1=8192
       CALL DIAG(RX,RX1,RY,RY1,&10)
       GOTO 20
10     RY=8192
20     CONTINUE

EBCASC - translate EBCDIC character string to ASCII
       CALL EBCASC(LENGTH,BUFFER1<,BUFFER2>)
       where
       LENGTH is the length in bytes of the EBCDIC string to be translated.
       BUFFER1 is  source string.  If  BUFFER2 is  not specified,  it  will also
              contain the output ASCII string.  BUFFER1 should be DIMENSIONed to
              be at least LENGTH bytes long.
       BUFFER2  is an  optional  string to  contain the  ASCII  output and,   if
              specified, should be at least length bytes long.

FSCLOS - close an open disk file.
       CALL FSCLOS(FID,RC,&?)
       where
       FID is a REAL*8 array of  at least 3 elements.  The first  element is the
              filename, the second is the filetype and the third is the filemode
              letter and number (e.g. 'a1')
       RC is the return code from CMS.
       &? is an optional alternate return taken when RC <> 0.

FSERAS - erase a disk file.
       CALL FSERAS(FID,RC,&?)
       where
       FID is a REAL*8 array of  at least 3 elements.  The first  element is the
              filename, the second is the filetype and the third is the filemode
              letter and number (e.g. 'a1').
       RC is the return code from CMS.
       &? is an optional alternate return taken when RC <> 0.

FSREAD - read a record from a disk file into a buffer.
       CALL FSREAD(FID,BUFFER,LENGTH,RECFM,RECNO,NOREC,RLEN,RC,&?)
       where
       FID is a REAL*8 array of  at least 3 elements.  The first  element is the
              filename, the second is the filetype and the third is the filemode
              letter and number (e.g. 'a1').
       BUFFER is the array to contain the record(s) read.
       LENGTH is the length of BUFFER in bytes.
       RECFM is either 'F' for fixed length records or 'V' for variable.
       RECNO is is the integer record number at which to start reading.   Giving
              a RECNO of zero means to read the next record.
       NOREC is the number of records to read.   Giving a NOREC which is greater
              than  one  implies  that  BUFFER will  be  contain  NOREC  logical
              records, i.e., blocked input/output.
       RLEN is the actual length of data read.
       RC contains the return code from cms.
       &? is an optional alternate return taken when RC <> 0.

FSSTAT - determine if a disk file exists.
       CALL FSSTAT(FID,NREC,RECFM,LRECL,AFID,RC,?&)
       where
       FID is a REAL*8 array of  at least 3 elements.  The first  element is the
              filename, the second is the filetype and the third is the filemode
              letter and number (e.g. 'a1').
       NREC is the record count for the file (if it exists).
       RECFM will contain  either 'F' for fixed  length record files or  'V' for
              variable.
       LRECL is the length of the longest record in the file.
       AFID is a  REAL*8 array  of at least  3 elements  which will  contain the
              filename,  filetype and filemode of the  data set found by FSSTAT.
              This allows use of an asterisk for one or more elements of FID.
       RC will contain
          0 if the file is found on a disk accessed in r/w mode.
          1 if it  is is found on  a disk accessed as  a r/o extension of  a r/w
          disk.
          2 if the file is found on a disk accessed in r/o mode.
          3 if it  is is found on  a disk accessed as  a r/o extension of  a r/o
          disk.
          20 if there is an invalid character in FID.
          24 if there is an invalid filemode.
          28 if the file specified in FID is not found.
          36 if the disk specified in FID is not accessed.
       &? is an optional alternate return taken when RC <> 0.

FSWRIT - write to a disk file from a buffer.
       CALL FSWRIT(FID,BUFFER,LENGTH,RECFM,RECNO,NOREC,RC,&?)
       where
       FID is a REAL*8 array of  at least 3 elements.  The first  element is the
              filename, the second is the filetype and the third is the filemode
              letter and number (e.g. 'a1')
       BUFFER is the array to be written.
       LENGTH is the length of BUFFER in bytes.
       RECFM is either 'F' for fixed length records or 'V' for variable.
       RECNO is is the integer record number at which to start writing.   Giving
              a RECNO of zero means to start writing at the next record.
       NOREC is the number of records to write.  Giving a NOREC which is greater
              than one implies  that BUFFER will be divided  into NOREC records,
              i.e., blocked input/output.
       RC contains the return code from cms.
       &? is an optional alternate return taken when RC <> 0.

IADDR - return memory address of FORTRAN variable.
       I=IADDR(variablename)
       This  may be  used in  conjunction with  DIAG or  SVC202 routines.    The
       variable may be  a scalar or array or  array element and may  reside in a
       COMMON block.

PUTSCR - semi-fullscreen output to 3270 terminals.
       CALL PUTSCR(SCREEN, LENGTH, .TRUE./.FALSE., LINNUM)
       Where
       SCREEN is an array with the screen in character format.  The only control
              characters allowed are 3270 start field (SF)  commands.  The lines
              should be padded to the linewidth of the screen with blanks.
       LENGTH is the length in characters of the screen image.
       .TRUE./.FALSE.   determine whether the screen will be erased prior to the
              write  operation (it  is  advised to  erase  the  screen on  first
              write).
       LINNUM is the line to  start display on - first line  is line 1.  Display
              may not fall through to the  command input area,  though a display
              directly on the command input area is permitted.

QUR    Query Unit Record
       CALL QUR(CUU, LINE, LENGTH, &1, &2). where:
       QUR will create a cp command line  which,  when executed,  will reset the
       spooling characteristics  of the  UR device  at the  supplied address  to
       their current state (at QUR invocation time).

       CUU    is address of virtual unit record device (x'00e' etc.).
       LINE   is output  array of length LENGTH  which will hold the  cp command
              generated by QUR.
       LENGTH is on input total  length of LINE and on output  length of command
              which resides in LINE.
       The two possible alternate returns are:
       1      the  array LINE  was to  small to  hold the  entire command;   its
              contents are undefined.
       2      the device at CUU either doesn't exist or is not a UR.

RAN9   To obtain a uniform random number
       X = RAN9(0)   (for real numbers in the range 0. to 1.)
       I = RAN9(0)   (for integers in the range 0 to 2**31-1)
           The function RAN9 must be declared to be of type integer in a FORTRAN
           calling program if integer results are desired.
       An entire array  can be filled with  random numbers with one  call to the
       routine as follows:
       CALL ARAN9(ARRAY,NUMBER) (to store "NUMBER" real values
                                 in ARRAY)
       CALL IRAN9(IARRAY,NUMBER) (to store "NUMBER" integers
                                  in IARRAY)
       Both the fixed point integer result and  the floating point result may be
       obtained with one call as follows:
              COMMON /XRAN9/XDUMMY(2),FRAN9,NRAN9   (FORTRAN)
              CALL PRAN9
              After the call to PRAN9,  FRAN9 and NRAN9 will contain the updated
              random numbers.
       One may  initialize a  sequence of random  numbers with  his own  seed as
       follows:
       CALL RAN9A(INIT)   (INIT is any positive non-zero integer)

RDTAPE FORTRAN interface to CMS RDTAPE macro.
       CALL RDTAPE(BUFFER,LENGTH,DEVICE,MODE,RLEN,RC,&?)
       where
       BUFFER is an array to which will  receive the block read.   BUFFER should
              be  DIMENSIONed in  the  calling program  sufficient  to hold  the
              maximum possible block and should be at least LENGTH bytes long.
       LENGTH is the length of the largest block to be read.
       DEVICE is 'TAP1', 'TAP2', 'TAP3' or 'TAP4' representing virtual addresses
              181-184.
       MODE is an integer  variable set by the calling program  according to the
              following table:

                       9 TRACK                 7 TRACK
                  6250 |1600 | 800         800 | 556 | 200| TRTCH
                  _________________        _______________________
                   211 | 195 | 203         187   123    59     OT
                                           179   115    51     O
                                           171   107    43     ET
                                           163    99    35     E
                                           147    83    19     OC

       RLEN is set by RDTAPE to the integer length of the block actually read.
       RC is set by RDTAPE to  the integer return code set by  the RDTAPE macro.
              The user  can specify that  return be  made to an  alternate label
              when RC is  non-zero,  by giving that label as  the 7th parameter.
              The following return codes are normal:
                    1 Invalid function or parameter list
                    2 End-of-file or end-of-tape
                    3 Permanent I/O error
                    4 Invalid device address
                    5 Tape not attached
                    8 Incorrect length error

SBREAK,EBREAK,TBREAK  FORTRAN interface  to  STAX  macro for  handling  terminal
       interrupts.
       Example:
       LOGICAL FUNCTION TBREAK
       .
       .
       CALL SBREAK
       .
       .
 10    CALL VTIME(I)
       IF(I.EQ.200000) GOTO 40
       IF(TBREAK(.TRUE.)) GOTO 20
       GOTO 10
 20    PRINT 30,I
       GOTO 10
 30    FORMAT(' ',I10)
       .
       .
 40    CALL EBREAK
       STOP
       END
       The  above program  will run  in an  infinite  loop until  20 seconds  of
       virtual CPU time have been used. If a console interrupt occurs during the
       run  the program  will  print  out the  virtual  CPU  used so  far.   The
       .TRUE./.FALSE.  in TBREAK is used to request the interrupt flag be turned
       off   or   left   in   its  present   condition.    EBREAK   stands   for
       End_BREAK_handling and SBREAK for Start_BREAK_handling, TBREAK stands for
       Test_BREAK_condition.

STIMER Is a delay routine.
       CALL STIMER(N)
       where N is number of hundredths of  a second to wait.  WARNING:  the user
       must 'CP SET TIMER REAL' before calling  stimer,  or he will be stuck and
       only '#CP IPL CMS' will get him out.

SCREEN - Fullscreen management routines for high-level languages.
       The following  routines are  defined in  this package:   SCREEN,  SCREND,
       SCRWRI, SCRREA, SCRSRC, SCRRCS.
SCREEN is used  to initialize  the fullscreen interrupt  handling and  to obtain
       information regarding the physical 327x  device.  Error returnes are made
       if device is not a 327x or interrupt handling failed.
       CALL SCREEN(RC,RDEV,W,H)
SCREND terminates interrupt handling for the 327x device.
       CALL SCREND(RC)
SCRWRI writes to the 327x device 1 to  8 arrays of characters+orders (the arrays
       will  be  data-chained  by  the program)   with  WRITE  or  ERASE/WRI  or
       ERASE/WRITE ALTERNATE. Error returnes in case of I/O error or CP grab the
       screen for a priority message in non-fullscreen mode.
       CALL SCRWRI(RC,FLAG,A,L,... );
SCRREA reads from the screen with READ BUFFER  or READ MODIFIED (the latt can be
       timed input - wait  n time or till interrupt from  terminal whichev comes
       first).
       CALL SCRREA(RC,FLAG,A,L)
SCRSRC converts from 327x  buffer address character pair to X,   Y coordina CALL
       SCRSRC(X,Y,S)
SCRRCS converts from X, Y coordinates to 327x buffer address character p
       CALL SCRRCS(X,Y,S)
       where
              RC is the INTEGER return code
                       Routine                            Alternate return
                       SCREEN:  0=No errors                            -
                                4=HNDINT error (1)                     1
                                8=HNDINT error (2)                     2
                               12=Invalid device type (not 327x)       3
                       SCREND:  0=No errors                            -
                                4=HNDINT error (3)                     1
                       SCRWRI:  0=No error                             -
                                4=CP grab                              1
                                8=Not operational                      2
                       SCRREA:  4,8: Same as SCRWRI                    1,2
                               12=Timer ran out, no read performed     3
                                0,-=Residual length in 2's complement    -
                                 (how many bytes were not read)
              RDEV Is real device data from DIAG X'24'
              W,H are INTEGER width and height of real (alternate) screen
              A is character array for I/O to terminal
              L is length of 'A'
              FLAG specifies operation
                          SCRWRI: >0 ERASE/WRITE ALTERNATE
                                  =0 ERASE/WRITE
                                  <0 WRITE
                          SCRREA: <0 READ BUFFER
                                  =0 READ MODIFIED upon interrupt from terminal
                                  >0 Wait FLAG 100's of a second before
                                     terminating READ MODIFIED no change to 'A')
              X and Y are INTEGER coordinates on screen, origin of '0'
              S is two byte area for buffer address
       SCRWRI will accept  up to eight DATA/LENGTH  parms and will use  CCW DATA
       CHAINING to write then as one to the console.  A 'WCC' Should be included
       only in  the first DATA/LENGTH pair.    for languages that  don't support
       variable  number of  parms e.g.   PASCAL  there are  alternate entries  -
       SCRWRI1 .. SCRWRI8 for 1 to 8 pairs of DATA/LENGTH.

SPM - disable  interrupts
       CALL SPM
       Calling SPM will disable the following interrupts:
                    Fixed-Point overflow Exception
                    Decimal overflow Exception
                    Exponent Underflow Exception
                    Significance Exception

SVC202 - interface to CMS SVC 202.
       CALL SVC202(R0,R1,R15,&n)
       will use R0,R1  for SVC 202 set R15  to return code from  SVC and execute
       'RETURN 1'  if R15 <>  0.   Use  together with 'IADDR'  routine described
       earlier.

TAPCTL interface to CMS TAPECTL macro.
       CALL TAPCTL(FUNCTION,DEVICE,MODE,RC,&?)
         where function is one of the following:
                'REW' - rewind
                'RUN' - rewind and unload
                 ERG' - erase record gap
                'WTM' - write tape mark (end-of-file)
                'BSR' - backspace record
                'BSF' - backspace file
                'FSR' - forward space record
                'FSF' - forward space file
         See RDTAPE for meaning of other parameters.

VTIME  - return virtual time used by job.
       CALL VTIME(I)

       Calling  VTIME  will  return  in  I  the   value  of  the  time  used  in
       milliseconds.   VTIME may  also be declared an INTEGER  FUNCTION and used
       so:
       I=VTIME(J)
       where J should be an integer variable.
       VTIME may return ascending or descending values,  (but the the order will
       be constant for one run. (Use IABS to get positive difference).

WRTAPE interface to CMS WRTAPE macro.
       CALL WRTAPE(BUFFER,LENGTH,DEVICE,MODE,RC,&?)
       where BUFFER is the array of LENGTH bytes to be written.
       See RDTAPE for description of other parameters.
       Return code 6 may  be encountered if trying to write  to a file protected
       tape.










































